---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeTerminal from "../../components/CodeTerminal.astro";
import KeyboardControls from "../../components/KeyboardControls.astro";
import { getDocsNav } from "../../data/nav";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const current = `${base}docs/cli/`;
const nav = getDocsNav(base);
---

<DocsLayout
  title="CLI Commands - fuku"
  description="Command reference for the fuku CLI."
  current={current}
  nav={nav}
  section="docs"
  breadcrumb={[{ label: "Docs", href: `${base}docs/` }, { label: "CLI Commands", href: current }]}
>
  <span class="kicker">// docs &middot; cli</span>
  <h1>CLI Commands</h1>
  <p class="lead">Command reference, flags, TUI controls, and environment file loading.</p>

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Reference</div>
  <h2>Commands</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Command</th>
        <th>Alias</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku run [profile]</code></td>
        <td><code>r</code></td>
        <td>Run services for a profile (default if omitted); refuses to start beside an instance already serving the same directory</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku stop [profile]</code></td>
        <td><code>s</code></td>
        <td>Stop services by killing processes in service directories</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku logs [services...]</code></td>
        <td><code>l</code></td>
        <td>Stream logs from a running fuku instance, or print a bounded slice of its buffer with <code>--no-follow</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku doctor [profile]</code></td>
        <td>&mdash;</td>
        <td>Diagnose configuration, environment, topology, and runtime issues</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku init</code></td>
        <td><code>i</code></td>
        <td>Generate a <code>fuku.yaml</code> template</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku version</code></td>
        <td><code>-v</code>, <code>--version</code></td>
        <td>Show version</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fuku help</code></td>
        <td><code>-h</code>, <code>--help</code></td>
        <td>Show help</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Options</div>
  <h2>Flags</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Flag</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--config</code>, <code>-c</code></td>
        <td>Path to config file (default: <code>fuku.yaml</code>, falls back to <code>fuku.yml</code>). Disables automatic override file merging. Supported with <code>run</code>, <code>stop</code>, <code>logs</code>, and <code>doctor</code> commands</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--no-ui</code></td>
        <td>Run without the interactive TUI (log-only mode)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--profile</code></td>
        <td>Filter by profile (used with <code>logs</code> command)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--tail</code></td>
        <td>Replay only the newest N buffered lines, clamped to the buffer size (used with <code>logs</code> command)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--since</code></td>
        <td>Replay only lines buffered within a duration such as <code>30s</code> or <code>5m</code> (used with <code>logs</code> command)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--no-follow</code></td>
        <td>Print the replayed lines and exit instead of following the live stream (used with <code>logs</code> command)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--summary</code></td>
        <td>Print a compact one-line-per-check report (used with <code>doctor</code> command)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--json</code></td>
        <td>Print the report as machine-readable JSON (used with <code>doctor</code> command)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Flags can be placed in any position:</p>
  <CodeTerminal code={`fuku run core --no-ui
fuku --no-ui run core        # Same result`} />

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Examples</div>
  <h2>Usage examples</h2>

  <CodeTerminal code={`# Run with TUI (default profile)
fuku

# Run a specific profile without TUI
fuku run core --no-ui

# Use short aliases
fuku r core

# Stop services for a profile
fuku stop                       # Default profile
fuku stop core                  # Specific profile

# Stream logs from running instance (separate terminal)
fuku logs                       # All services
fuku logs api auth              # Specific services
fuku logs --profile core api    # Filter by profile
fuku l api db                   # Short alias

# Read the buffer without following it
fuku logs --tail 50 --no-follow api
fuku logs --since 5m --no-follow

# Use a custom config file
fuku --config path/to/fuku.yaml run core
fuku -c custom.yaml run core

# Diagnose configuration, environment, and runtime issues
fuku doctor                     # Default profile
fuku doctor core                # Specific profile
fuku doctor --summary           # Compact one-line-per-check report
fuku doctor --json              # Machine-readable report

# Generate config template
fuku init

# Show help / version
fuku help
fuku version`} />

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Instances</div>
  <h2>One instance per project</h2>

  <p>
    Startup preflight stops processes whose working directory matches a selected service, so a second
    <code>fuku run</code> in the same directory would stop the services the first one is running. To prevent that,
    every instance publishes the project directory it serves, and <code>fuku run</code> exits <code>1</code> when
    another instance is already serving the same directory &mdash; whatever profile either one uses.
  </p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`$ fuku run core
Error: fuku is already running for this project (API on 127.0.0.1:9876)
Read its output with 'fuku logs', or stop it before starting another instance`} />

  <p>
    Identity is published on the API. <a href={`${base}docs/api/#get-live`}>GET /api/v1/live</a> needs no token and
    returns <code>product</code>, an <code>instance</code> id, and a <code>project</code> fingerprint &mdash; a hash
    rather than the path, because the endpoint is unauthenticated. The authenticated
    <a href={`${base}docs/api/#get-status`}>GET /api/v1/status</a> returns the same instance id along with the
    absolute project directory. The same fingerprint travels in the log socket banner, so
    <code>fuku logs</code> flags a stream that belongs to another project.
  </p>

  <p>
    The check reads the API, so it only applies when <code>server.listen</code> is configured. Instances without an
    API cannot be detected, and a second run will preflight-stop the first one's services.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Logs</div>
  <h2>fuku logs</h2>

  <p>
    A running instance keeps the most recent <strong>5000</strong> lines of service output in memory.
    <code>fuku logs</code> connects to that instance over its profile socket, replays the buffer, then follows
    the live stream until interrupted.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>--tail</code>, <code>--since</code>, and <code>--no-follow</code> bound that replay. The two bounds
    combine, and <code>--no-follow</code> prints the selected lines and exits <code>0</code> instead of staying
    attached &mdash; which is what makes the command usable from scripts, CI, and AI agents that need log
    evidence without holding a process open.
  </p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`# Newest 50 lines from one service, then exit
fuku logs --tail 50 --no-follow api

# Everything buffered in the last 5 minutes, then exit
fuku logs --since 5m --no-follow

# Both bounds, across two services
fuku logs --tail 20 --since 30s --no-follow api auth`} />

  <p>
    The bounds apply to the replay with or without <code>--no-follow</code>, so <code>fuku logs --tail 20</code>
    starts from the newest 20 buffered lines and follows from there. The same buffer is readable over HTTP from
    <a href={`${base}docs/api/#list-logs`}>GET /api/v1/logs</a>.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Diagnostics</div>
  <h2>fuku doctor</h2>

  <p>
    <code>fuku doctor</code> runs a read-only health check and prints a sectioned
    report &mdash; <strong>Environment</strong>, <strong>Configuration</strong>,
    <strong>Services</strong>, <strong>Topology</strong>, and <strong>Runtime</strong>.
    It diagnoses a broken setup without starting any services, so it works even when
    the config fails to load. See <a href={`${base}docs/troubleshooting/#diagnostics`}>Troubleshooting</a> for what each section checks.
  </p>

  <p>Each check carries a status glyph:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Glyph</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>✓</code></td><td>ok</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>○</code></td><td>idle (inactive but expected)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>↑</code></td><td>note (informational)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⚠</code></td><td>warning (non-fatal)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>✗</code></td><td>failure</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The exit code makes <code>doctor</code> usable in scripts and CI:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Exit code</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>No failures (warnings and notes are allowed)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>At least one check failed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>3</code></td><td>doctor itself errored (e.g. failed to write the JSON report)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Keyboard</div>
  <h2>TUI controls</h2>

  <p>When running with the interactive TUI (default), use these keyboard shortcuts:</p>

  <KeyboardControls />

  <hr />

  <div class="section-eyebrow" id="environment-file-loading">Environment</div>
  <h2>Environment file loading</h2>

  <p>On startup, fuku loads its own environment files using <a href="https://github.com/joho/godotenv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">godotenv</a>, in this order (later files take precedence):</p>

  <ol>
    <li><code>.env</code></li>
    <li><code>.env.&lt;GO_ENV&gt;</code></li>
    <li><code>.env.&lt;GO_ENV&gt;.local</code></li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    These files configure the fuku process itself. Each service inherits fuku's environment but does <strong>not</strong> receive any per-service variables injected by fuku &mdash; child processes run in their service <code>dir</code> and are responsible for loading their own <code>.env</code> files.
  </p>

  <p>
    Per-service <code>.env</code> files are still read by fuku, but only for display in the TUI's <a href={`${base}features/tui/#service-info`}>info aside</a> (the <code>env</code> tab). By default fuku reads <code>.env</code>, <code>.env.local</code>, <code>.env.development</code>, and <code>.env.development.local</code> from each service's <code>dir</code>; override the list with the per-service <code>env.files</code> field (see <a href={`${base}docs/configuration/#services`}>Configuration</a>).
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
